+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Thin Objects:** The algorithm produces errors with thin objects because the neighborhood sampling may not accurately represent the surface curvature or occlusion for structures thinner than the sampling radius [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The method is slow due to the triple nested loops for neighbor sampling for every voxel [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** Voxels near the grid boundary require special handling (skipping or padding) to avoid out-of-bounds access [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Compensation Map
-A nonlinear rescaling technique applied to JAOCC results to mitigate errors from artificial multiple occlusion [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Definition:** Rescales computed AO for highly occluded regions using a 1D luminance texture encoded on the GPU. It automatically clamps input AO to $[0, 1]$ [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Purpose:** Corrects overdarkening caused by objects thinner than $\delta$ being in proximity closer than $\delta$, where the occlusion volume of one object passes entirely through another [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Parameter:** The curve is fitted using AOV results from multiple viewpoints of reference scenes (Suburb, House) against ray-traced results. The right end of the curve is set based on the intuition that a corner with three walls at a right angle has ~75% occlusion [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Pitfall:** The compensation map has no physical basis; it is an ad hoc solution for scenes violating the $\delta$-thickness assumption. AO for such scenes is strictly over-estimated, and the correction is roughly parabolic [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The additional texture fetch impact is below the threshold of measurement [S4, unofficial].
+A nonlinear rescaling technique applied to JAOCC results to mitigate "artificial multiple occlusion" errors [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Definition:** A 1D luminance texture (curve) applied to the computed AO factor. It rescales highly occluded regions to correct over-darkening [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Mechanism:** The curve is fitted to ray-traced reference data from specific scenes (e.g., Suburb, House). It automatically clamps input AO to $[0, 1]$. The right end of the curve is set based on the intuition that a corner with three walls at right angles has ~75% occlusion [S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to Choose:** Use when rendering scenes with objects thinner than the voxel grid resolution $\delta$ in proximity closer than $\delta$, which causes artificial multiple occlusion (overdarkening of adjacent faces) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Comparison:** Unlike the base JAOCC algorithm, the compensation map has no physical basis; it is an ad-hoc correction for scenes violating the $\delta$-thickness assumption. The error it corrects is strictly over-estimated AO, typically worse in highly occluded areas [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** The additional texture fetch cost is below the threshold of measurement [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot (2 \cdot \text{count} + 1)^3)$, where $N$ is the number of non-empty voxels. The cubic neighborhood search makes it expensive for large `count` values [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(X \cdot Y \cdot Z)$ for the voxel grid. The compensation map adds $O(K)$ memory for a 1D texture of size $K$ [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The algorithm is noted as slow [S3, unofficial]. The compensation map's performance impact is negligible [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces smooth results for higher neighbor counts; robust to tessellation and level of detail changes [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. Dynamically determines normals to avoid hard lighting transitions at AABB edges [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Errors with thin objects; artificial multiple occlusion artifacts in dense, thin structures; slow computation [S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When smooth ambient occlusion is desired for voxel-based scenes and computational cost is acceptable. Useful for scenes where fixed AABB normals cause visible artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For scenes with many thin objects or high-frequency geometry where the $\delta$-thickness assumption is violated, unless the compensation map is applied [S4, unofficial]. For real-time applications requiring high performance, this method may be too slow [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot (2 \cdot count + 1)^3)$, where $N$ is the number of solid voxels. The cubic neighborhood sampling makes it significantly slower than simple neighbor counting [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space per voxel if computed in-place, or $O(N)$ for the output grid.
+*   **Strengths:** Produces smooth shading results for higher neighbor counts; avoids hard AABB edge lighting artifacts by using local geometry [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Robust to tessellation changes [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow performance; inaccurate for thin objects; the "depth" parameter is empirical [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When visual smoothness is prioritized over performance and objects are sufficiently thick relative to the voxel grid.
+*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications with large voxel grids or scenes with thin geometry (e.g., fences, wires) without the compensation map.
 
 ## Background
-JAOCC Shading originates from voxel-based rendering techniques aimed at improving the visual quality of voxel landscapes. It addresses the specific issue of hard lighting transitions at voxel edges, which are physically implausible due to the fixed normals of axis-aligned bounding boxes [S1, unofficial]. The method was implemented in various voxel engine projects, such as `doommy` and `Minecraft` clones, often as a post-processing or pre-computation step to enhance shading [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The concept of using neighbor counting for ambient occlusion in voxel grids is also related to Ambient Occlusion Volumes (AOV) by McGuire et al., which introduced the compensation map to handle thin geometry artifacts [S4, unofficial].
+JAOCC Shading originates from voxel rendering implementations in open-source projects and academic theses focusing on voxel-based lighting [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The specific weighted neighbor counting method is documented in the `doommy` project [S3, unofficial]. The concept of using local neighborhood to determine normals and avoid AABB artifacts is discussed in a thesis on raycasting-based voxel rendering [S1, unofficial]. The "Compensation Map" variant was introduced by McGuire in "Ambient Occlusion Volumes" (2010) to address physical inaccuracies in voxel AO [S4, unofficial].
 
